##  ER Diagram (mermaid source — paste into any mermaid renderer, or keep as docs/erd.mmd)

```mermaid
erDiagram
  CANTEENS ||--o{ MENU_ITEMS : offers
  CANTEENS ||--o{ ORDERS : receives
  USERS ||--o{ ORDERS : places
  ORDERS ||--o{ ORDER_ITEMS : contains
  MENU_ITEMS ||--o{ ORDER_ITEMS : referenced_in

  USERS {
    int id PK
    string full_name
    string username UK
    string email UK
    string password_hash
    string role "student | staff | admin"
    timestamp created_at
  }

  CANTEENS {
    int id PK
    string name
    string location
    boolean is_active
    timestamp created_at
  }

  MENU_ITEMS {
    int id PK
    int canteen_id FK
    string name
    string description
    decimal price
    string category "snacks | meals | drinks"
    boolean is_available
    timestamp created_at
  }

  ORDERS {
    int id PK
    int user_id FK
    int canteen_id FK
    int token_number
    string status "placed | preparing | ready"
    decimal subtotal
    decimal handling_fee
    decimal total_amount
    timestamp created_at
  }

  ORDER_ITEMS {
    int id PK
    int order_id FK
    int menu_item_id FK
    string item_name_snapshot
    decimal price_snapshot
    int quantity
    decimal subtotal
  }
```

### Design notes
- `order_items` snapshots `item_name_snapshot` / `price_snapshot` at order time — order history must never change retroactively if a menu item's price or name changes later.
- `orders.canteen_id` exists from Day 1 even though Phase 1 runs effectively one canteen — this makes Phase 3's multi-canteen work additive rather than a schema migration.
- `token_number` is a plain sequential int for now. Per-canteen-per-day reset logic is explicitly out of scope until Phase 3 (see NFR/FR16 in the roadmap) — don't build it early, it'll just be extra surface area to re-test later.
- Indexes to add when you write the Alembic migration (Day 3–4): unique on `users.username`, unique on `users.email`, index on `menu_items(canteen_id, category)`, index on `orders(canteen_id, status)`, index on `orders(user_id)`, index on `order_items(order_id)`.
- No `carts` table — cart lives entirely in frontend state (Zustand/Context) per FR6/NFR6. Only the price gets re-validated server-side at order time. Don't persist cart server-side in Phase 1; it's unnecessary complexity for a single-session flow.

